## 1. Product Theme & Vision

**One-line theme:** *"The fastest, most trustworthy way to match a vacant room or apartment with someone who needs one."*

Think of the product like a **matchmaking counter at a busy train station**, a mini real-estate agency. Landlords drop off a listing — a single room or a full apartment — tenants walk up with a filter in hand ("under this price, near this area, has Wi-Fi, apartment not room-share"), and the app's only job is to get the right two people talking to each other quickly and safely. Everything in the MVP should serve that single transaction — listing → discovery → contact — and nothing else. Payments, background checks, and in-app messaging are "later counters" we deliberately don't build yet.

**Design principle for every feature decision:** *"Does this help a room or apartment get matched faster or more safely? If not, cut it from MVP."*

### Visual Brand Theme

Primary palette: **olive green and white.** Olive reads as grounded, natural, and trustworthy without leaning into the generic fintech-blue or real-estate-red clichés most rental platforms default to — it also has enough contrast range to carry both a calm marketing surface and a legible, high-contrast product UI.

| Token | Hex | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| Olive Primary | `#6B7A3A` | Primary buttons, active nav state, links, key icons |
| Olive Deep | `#4A5A2A` | Hover/pressed states, header background option, emphasis text |
| Olive Muted | `#A8B589` | Secondary backgrounds, tags/badges, subtle borders |
| White | `#FFFFFF` | Primary background, card surfaces, negative space |
| Off-White | `#F7F7F2` | Section backgrounds to break up pure white without introducing a new hue |
| Charcoal Text | `#2B2B26` | Body copy, headings — warm-black rather than pure #000 to stay consistent with the olive undertone |

White should dominate as the base surface (cards, backgrounds) with olive reserved for actionable/brand moments — buttons, active states, the header, and the verified-badge accent — so the palette reads clean and trustworthy rather than heavy. Avoid using olive as a full-bleed background on more than one section per page; it's a signal color, not a wallpaper.

