## 6. Techstack Recommendation

Think of the stack like **choosing a delivery bike vs. a truck before you know your delivery volume** — you want something fast to build, cheap to run, and easy to swap parts of later without a full rebuild. That rules out a fully custom native-mobile-first build for MVP (too slow for validation) in favor of a lean, code-based web stack you can iterate on quickly.

### Recommended Stack

| Layer | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Frontend (web)** | Next.js 15 (React) + Tailwind CSS | Server-rendered for SEO on listing pages (tenants will Google "rooms near X" or "apartments near X"), fast to build UI, App Router handles both landlord dashboard and tenant search in one codebase |
| **Mobile** | Responsive PWA first, not native | Skip App Store review cycles for MVP speed; wrap in Capacitor later if native feel is needed |
| **Backend** | Go (Golang) — using a lightweight router (chi or Gin) + REST | Compiled, statically typed, excellent concurrency model for handling many simultaneous search/contact requests cheaply; produces a single static binary that's trivial to deploy and cheap to run at scale compared to an interpreted runtime |
| **Database access (Go)** | sqlc (compile-time-checked SQL) or GORM | sqlc is the leaner, more idiomatic-Go choice — write raw SQL, get type-safe Go structs generated; use GORM only if the team wants a full ORM's convenience over sqlc's precision |
| **Database** | PostgreSQL (via Supabase or Neon) | Relational data (listings, users, matches) fits SQL well; Supabase also gives you managed auth + storage out of the box even though the API layer is Go, cutting infra build time significantly |
| **File/Photo Storage** | Supabase Storage or Cloudflare R2 | Cheap, S3-compatible, accessed from the Go backend via standard S3-compatible SDK calls, no need for custom CDN logic |
| **Auth** | Supabase Auth (JWT-based) | Don't build OTP/verification from scratch — buy this; the Go backend verifies Supabase-issued JWTs on incoming requests rather than owning auth itself, keeping the auth surface language-agnostic |
| **Search/Filters** | Postgres with PostGIS extension (for location radius search), queried from Go via sqlc | Avoid Elasticsearch complexity until listing volume justifies it |
| **Payments (Phase 2)** | Paystack / Flutterwave (if targeting Nigeria/Africa) or Stripe (if global), integrated server-side from Go | Pick based on pilot market — this determines a lot of your compliance surface |
| **Hosting** | Vercel (frontend) + Railway/Render/Fly.io (Go backend as a compiled binary/Docker image) | A single static Go binary is one of the cheapest, simplest things to containerize and deploy — minimal DevOps overhead for a 2–3 person team |
| **Analytics** | PostHog (self-host optional) | Tracks the exact funnel metrics you need (listing → view → contact) |

