## 6a. Landing Page (Marketing + Auth Entry Point)

The landing page is the platform's front door — it has to do two jobs at once: **sell the idea in under 10 seconds** to someone who's never heard of the product, and **funnel them into signup/login** without making them dig for it. Think of it like a **storefront window that's also the entrance** — the display has to communicate what's inside at a glance, and the door has to be right there, not around the back.

Visual treatment follows the palette defined in §1: white/off-white backgrounds throughout, olive reserved for the header, primary CTAs (Sign Up, Find a Place, List a Place), and the verified-badge accent — so the two buttons that matter most are the only things that pull the eye.

### Goals
- Explain what the platform is and who it's for (landlord vs. tenant, room vs. apartment) within the first screen
- Get a first-time visitor to sign up, or a returning user to log in, in one click from anywhere on the page
- Route landlords and tenants toward the correct next step (they have different goals: "list a place" vs. "find a place", and different property types within each)

### Page Structure (top to bottom)

| Section | Purpose | Content |
|---|---|---|
| **Header (persistent)** | Constant access to auth | Logo, nav links (How it works, For Landlords, For Tenants), **Log In** button, **Sign Up** button (primary CTA styling) |
| **Hero** | Communicate the core value prop instantly | Headline (e.g., "Find your next room or apartment. Or fill your next vacancy.") + subheadline explaining the platform covers both room-shares and full apartments + two CTA buttons: "Find a Place" (tenant signup path) and "List a Place" (landlord signup path) |
| **Dual value prop section** | Speak to both personas without confusing them | Two-column split: "For Tenants" (search and filter by room or apartment, verified listings) vs. "For Landlords" (list free, reach serious tenants, manage from a dashboard, list a room or a whole unit) |
| **How it works** | Reduce friction/uncertainty for first-time visitors | 3-step visual: Sign up → Search or List → Connect directly (mirrors the flows in §7) |
| **Trust signals** | Address the "is this legit" hesitation before asking for signup | Verified profiles badge explainer, "Report a listing" mention, number of active listings/landlords once you have real numbers (omit or use qualitative copy pre-launch — never fabricate stats) |
| **Social proof (post-pilot only)** | Credibility once you have it | Testimonial quotes or a simple stat bar (e.g., "X rooms listed in [pilot city]") — leave this section out entirely at launch rather than fake it |
| **Secondary CTA / footer band** | Catch scrollers who didn't convert at the hero | Repeat "Sign Up Free" CTA, plus footer links (About, Contact, Terms, Privacy) |

### Login / Sign-Up Placement & Flow

- **Persistent header CTA:** "Log In" (secondary/text style) and "Sign Up" (primary button) visible on every scroll position, not just the top — sticky header.
- **Two sign-up entry points, one underlying flow:** "Find a Place" and "List a Place" both lead to the same signup form, but pre-select the account type (tenant vs. landlord) so the post-signup redirect sends them to search vs. the listing-creation form respectively. Property type (room vs. apartment) is chosen inside that next step, not on the landing page — don't build two separate signup forms per property type, one form/one toggle for account type is enough.
- **Signup fields (MVP-minimal):** Name, phone number, email, password (or passwordless OTP-only, consistent with §9's OTP verification approach). Do not ask for anything beyond what's needed to verify identity at this stage — every extra field is a drop-off point.
- **Login modal vs. full page:** Recommend a modal/overlay for login (keeps user on the landing page context) rather than a full page redirect, since login is a low-friction action that shouldn't feel like leaving the site.
- **No-login browsing preserved:** Per the tenant flow in §7.2, tenants can still browse/search without signing up — the landing page CTA sells signup as the way to *unlock contacting landlords and saving listings*, not as a gate to basic browsing. This keeps the funnel honest: show value first, then ask for signup at the natural conversion moment.

### Copy Direction

Write from the visitor's side of the screen, not the platform's. Lead with what they get ("Find a verified room or apartment near you" / "List your room or apartment to serious tenants"), not how the system works ("Our AI-powered matching engine..."). Avoid generic marketing filler — the copy should sound like it's specific to your pilot city and to the room-vs-apartment distinction tenants actually search on, not a template that could describe any rental app.

