## 6b. System Design & Architecture

This is the architecture the codebase must follow from the first commit — not a suggestion to revisit later. Think of it like **pouring a building's foundation before anyone argues about paint colors**: the folder structure, layering, and request path below should be non-negotiable in code review, even while feature scope flexes.

### 6b.1 High-Level Architecture

```
                        ┌─────────────────────┐
                        │   Next.js Frontend    │
                        │  (Vercel, SSR/ISR)     │
                        └──────────┬───────────┘
                                   │ HTTPS/JSON (REST)
                                   ▼
                        ┌─────────────────────┐
                        │   Go API Service      │
                        │  (chi/Gin, stateless)  │
                        │  containerized binary  │
                        └──────────┬───────────┘
                     ┌─────────────┼─────────────────┐
                     ▼             ▼                 ▼
            ┌────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐  ┌───────────────┐
            │ PostgreSQL  │ │ Supabase Auth │  │ Object Storage │
            │ + PostGIS   │ │ (JWT issuer)  │  │ (Supabase/R2)  │
            └────────────┘ └──────────────┘  └───────────────┘
```

**Key principle:** the Go service is stateless and horizontally scalable — no session state lives in the API process itself. Every request carries its own auth (JWT) and the API can be scaled by running more identical containers behind a load balancer with zero coordination between them.

### 6b.2 Backend Architecture Pattern — Layered / Clean Architecture

Think of this like a **restaurant's kitchen line** — the person taking orders (handler) never touches the stove (repository), and the person cooking (service/business logic) never deals with the customer directly. Each station only knows about the station immediately next to it. This is what makes it possible to swap one station out (e.g., change databases) without rebuilding the whole kitchen.

Four layers, strict one-directional dependency (outer layers depend on inner ones, never the reverse):

1. **Handler/Transport layer** — parses HTTP requests, validates input shape, calls a service, writes the HTTP response. Contains zero business logic.
2. **Service layer** — owns business rules (e.g., "a listing can't go live without 3 photos," "a landlord past their free-trial limit can't publish"). Calls repositories, never touches `net/http` or SQL directly.
3. **Repository layer** — the only layer that talks to Postgres (via sqlc-generated queries). Returns domain structs, not raw DB rows.
4. **Domain layer** — plain Go structs and interfaces (Listing, User, Match) with no framework or infrastructure dependency at all. This is the layer every other layer depends on, and it depends on nothing.

This is deliberately **not** a single fat `main.go` with SQL inline in HTTP handlers — that pattern is fast for a weekend prototype and actively hostile to a team of more than one engineer or any future refactor (e.g., swapping Postgres for something else, or adding a second transport like gRPC).

### 6b.3 Required Codebase Structure

```
room-apartment-marketplace/
├── backend/                        # Go service
│   ├── cmd/
│   │   └── api/
│   │       └── main.go             # composition root — wires everything, nothing else
│   ├── internal/                   # not importable outside this module — enforces boundaries
│   │   ├── domain/                 # plain structs + interfaces: Listing, User, Match, PropertyType
│   │   ├── handler/                # HTTP handlers, grouped by resource
│   │   │   ├── listing_handler.go
│   │   │   ├── auth_handler.go
│   │   │   └── contact_handler.go
│   │   ├── service/                # business logic, one file per bounded concern
│   │   │   ├── listing_service.go
│   │   │   ├── search_service.go
│   │   │   └── contact_service.go
│   │   ├── repository/             # sqlc-generated + hand-wired data access
│   │   │   ├── listing_repo.go
│   │   │   └── user_repo.go
│   │   ├── middleware/             # auth (JWT verification), logging, rate limiting, CORS
│   │   └── config/                 # env var loading, typed config struct
│   ├── migrations/                 # versioned SQL migrations (golang-migrate)
│   ├── db/
│   │   ├── queries/                # raw .sql files sqlc compiles into Go
│   │   └── sqlc.yaml
│   ├── pkg/                        # code safe to share/import externally (rare at MVP)
│   ├── go.mod
│   └── Dockerfile
│
├── frontend/                       # Next.js app
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── (marketing)/            # landing page route group
│   │   ├── (auth)/                 # login/signup routes
│   │   ├── (dashboard)/            # landlord dashboard, authenticated
│   │   └── search/                 # tenant search + listing detail
│   ├── components/
│   │   ├── ui/                     # design-system primitives (buttons, cards) — olive/white tokens live here
│   │   └── features/               # feature-specific components (ListingCard, SearchFilters)
│   ├── lib/
│   │   ├── api-client.ts           # single typed client wrapping calls to the Go API
│   │   └── auth.ts
│   └── styles/
│       └── tokens.css              # olive/white palette as CSS variables — single source of truth
│
├── docs/
│   └── prd/                        # this PRD and future revisions
└── infra/                          # IaC / deploy configs (Railway/Fly.io/Vercel configs)
```

**Rule this structure enforces:** anything under `internal/` cannot be imported by another Go module — this is a Go language feature, not just convention, and it's what stops the domain layer from ever accidentally depending on an HTTP framework. Frontend and backend stay in one monorepo at MVP size (easier for a 2–3 person team to coordinate changes across the API boundary), split into separate repos only if/when team size or deploy cadence genuinely diverges.

### 6b.4 API Design Rules

- **REST, versioned from day one:** `/api/v1/...` — even a 2-person team benefits from this the first time a breaking change is needed post-launch.
- **Every endpoint validates input at the handler boundary** before it reaches the service layer — bad data never reaches business logic.
- **Errors are structured, not raw strings:** a consistent JSON error shape (`{ "error": { "code": "...", "message": "..." } }`) so the frontend can branch on `code`, not fragile string matching.
- **Property type (`room` | `apartment`) is a first-class field on the Listing domain struct**, validated at the domain layer so it's impossible to persist a listing without one, and both handler and repository layers simply pass it through.
- **Pagination and filtering are query-parameter driven** (`?type=apartment&min_price=...&lat=...&lng=...&radius_km=...`) — no POST-body search endpoints; keeps search requests cacheable and shareable as URLs.

### 6b.5 Cross-Cutting Concerns (non-negotiable from MVP)

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Auth** | JWT verification middleware on every protected route; public routes (search, browse) explicitly whitelisted, not the default |
| **Logging** | Structured (JSON) logging via `slog` (Go standard library) — every request logged with a request ID for traceability |
| **Config** | All config via environment variables, loaded once at startup into a typed struct — no scattered `os.Getenv` calls through the codebase |
| **Testing** | Service layer gets unit tests (business logic is what breaks silently); repository layer gets integration tests against a real Postgres test container; handlers get a thin layer of request/response contract tests |
| **CI/CD** | Every PR: lint (`golangci-lint`) + test + build, before merge; main branch auto-deploys backend (Docker image) and frontend (Vercel) independently |
| **Rate limiting** | Applied at the middleware layer on auth and contact-reveal endpoints specifically — these are the endpoints most exposed to abuse/scraping |
| **Database migrations** | Every schema change is a versioned migration file, never a manual production DB edit — this is what makes rollback possible |

### 6b.6 Why This Is "Superior" for This Specific Project

Not architecture for architecture's sake — each choice maps to a real constraint of this project: a small team, a marketplace that needs to scale search/contact traffic cheaply, and two property types that must share almost all logic while diverging on a few fields. The layered backend keeps the room-vs-apartment branching contained to the domain and service layers instead of leaking into every handler; the stateless Go service means scaling under listing-traffic spikes (e.g., a promo push in the pilot city) is just running more containers; and the strict `internal/` boundary means a future engineer joining the team can't accidentally create the kind of tangled dependencies that make marketplaces painful to maintain past year one.

